+def ages_bounding_time_held(facts: dict[str, Any]) -> tuple[int, int] | None:
+    """(age at inception, age now) when they bound an UNSTATED time held, else None.
+
+    "I took this policy out when I was 70 and I am 72 now" states no duration,
+    and whole ages do not give one: 70 and 72 is anything over one year and
+    under three. So nothing is written into the facts and no waiting period is
+    marked served from it. Putting "2 years" into the facts was measured twice:
+    the model refused pneumonia under a 36-month pre-existing diseases wait, and
+    with a rule against that, paid "my old heart trouble" one year in.
+
+    Two years apart or more, because 66 and 67 can be one day: the bound
+    "at least a year less" would then say nothing.
+
+    None when the person is known to have had the illness before the policy,
+    or for at least as long as the policy may have been held: that is a
+    history, and the one question these ages are used to ask is about an
+    illness with none.
+
+    None when a procedure is named. Specified-disease waiting periods list
+    mostly operations (cataract, hernia, joint replacement), and measured on
+    "61 then, 63 now, I need a hernia operation" the question below was
+    answered conditional, 3 samples of 3, while citing the 24-month period
+    as "unclear whether served".
+    """
+    age, start = facts.get("age"), facts.get("age_at_policy_start")
+    if duration_days(facts, "time_since_policy_start") is not None or stated(facts.get("procedure")):
+        return None
+    if not age or not start or age - start < 2:
+        return None
+    known = duration_days(facts, "condition_known_for")
+    if facts.get("pre_existing_condition") == "yes" or (
+        known is not None and known >= (age - start - 1) * _DAYS_PER_UNIT["years"]
+    ):
+        return None
+    return start, age
 
 
